From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 11:04:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] lmkd: end the cached app that holds the most host graphics
 memory

The graphics memory of a virtual device lives on the host, in a budget
that Android's memory accounting cannot see: PSI shows no stall while
the host's pool fills, and the host can only refuse allocations. The
guest's virtio-gpu driver keeps the host's last report of that budget
in /sys/class/drm/card0/gpu_memory_report and notifies the file when a
new report arrives.

liblmkd_gpu_reclaim (gpu_reclaim.cpp) holds the report format, the
choice of the victim and the limits. lmkd.cpp polls the file in its
epoll loop, with a timer that asks for a new report every 3 s while the
pressure lasts. Under pressure it ends the registered process that holds
the most host graphics memory (at least 32 MiB, idle for at least 10 s)
among the cached apps (oom_score_adj 900 and up), or among the previous
app's band (700 to 799) when no cached app qualifies. Nothing else is
ended, and there is no fallback to the RAM or LRU order: when no holder
qualifies, nothing happens. The pid must be one the activity manager
registered with lmkd, and /proc/<pid>/comm must be the name the host
gave. At most one kill every 3 s, three while one pressure episode
lasts, none while another kill is pending.

The kill goes through kill_one_process with the vendor kill reason 1000
("graphics memory pressure"), so the activity manager and statsd report
it as they report any other lmkd kill, and nothing else is shown.

ro.lmk.gpu_reclaim (default false) turns it on. Without it, or when the
file does not exist, nothing is opened and nothing changes. The unit
test lmkd_unit_test covers the parser, the choice and the limits.
